docs/adr/0289-marks-of-git-hosts-in-the-console.md	3. **Forgejo (owner).** The console shows the Forgejo logo. The license of that logo is CC BY-SA 4.0. The console shows the attribution that the license needs, and a link to the license. (owner)
docs/adr/0289-marks-of-git-hosts-in-the-console.md	4. **Only where we name the integration (owner).** A mark or an icon appears only where the console names the integration. One example is the page of a Git host connection. A mark is never decoration. (owner)
docs/adr/0290-console-themes-friendly-theme-dropped-and-the-search-shortcut.md	1. **No Friendly theme (owner).** The console has no Friendly theme. Default and Terminal stay (ADR 180 decision 1). (owner)
docs/adr/0290-console-themes-friendly-theme-dropped-and-the-search-shortcut.md	2. **Search shortcut in the Default theme (owner).** In the Default theme, Ctrl K (Cmd K on macOS) opens search. The console shows no shortcut hint for it. This amends ADR 180 decision 2: the Default theme has one console shortcut, and it has no hint. (owner)
docs/adr/0290-console-themes-friendly-theme-dropped-and-the-search-shortcut.md	4. **No Maple Mono (owner).** The console does not use the font Maple Mono. The fonts of ADR 180 decision 1 stay. (owner)
docs/adr/0291-insights-count-failures-by-token-and-service-principal.md	1. **The unit of a failure count (owner).** Insights count failures by access token and by service principal. They never count failures by person. (owner)
docs/adr/0291-insights-count-failures-by-token-and-service-principal.md	3. **Failure insights are opt-in (owner).** An installation shows failure insights only when an operator turns them on. The setting is off by default. Its effective value is in the API. A change writes an audit entry. (owner)
docs/adr/0292-plain-ldap-and-smtp-connections-are-refused-unless-an-operator-opts-in.md	1. **Refused by default (owner).** The server refuses an LDAP connection and an SMTP connection that has no TLS, except to a loopback address. (owner)
docs/adr/0292-plain-ldap-and-smtp-connections-are-refused-unless-an-operator-opts-in.md	2. **An explicit opt-in (owner).** An installation can allow plain connections with one setting. The setting is off by default. (owner)
docs/adr/0292-plain-ldap-and-smtp-connections-are-refused-unless-an-operator-opts-in.md	3. **The setting is visible (owner).** The API shows its effective value and its source. Each change writes an audit entry. The console shows the state in a quiet way, in the settings and in the status of the connection. It shows no warning banner. (owner)
docs/adr/0292-plain-ldap-and-smtp-connections-are-refused-unless-an-operator-opts-in.md	4. **The hosted service never turns it on (owner).** The hosted service does not allow the setting. (owner)
docs/adr/0293-the-stuck-hint-of-a-runner-job.md	1. **One rule (owner).** The hint "stuck" shows when a running job has had no output for 10 minutes. There is no other rule. There is no rule that compares the time with earlier jobs. (owner)
docs/adr/0293-the-stuck-hint-of-a-runner-job.md	2. **A setting with an effective value (owner).** The 10 minutes are a setting. The API shows its effective value and its source. A change writes an audit entry. (owner)
docs/adr/0293-the-stuck-hint-of-a-runner-job.md	3. **A hint, never a status (owner).** "Stuck" is a hint. It is never a status word. The status of the job does not change. The executor or a person changes the status. The hint does not. (owner)
docs/adr/0294-registry-used-by-evidence-needs-an-exact-version.md	1. **Exact inputs only (owner).** A provider version shows in "Used by" only when the Pulumi checkpoint has an exact `inputs.version` for it. Otherwise the page shows "Version not known". The server never guesses a version, for example from a lock file or from the newest release. (owner)
docs/adr/0294-registry-used-by-evidence-needs-an-exact-version.md	2. **Self-service request (owner).** A request in the catalog (Self-service) shows the exact program version, with a label that says it is the program version. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	1. **The route is allowed (owner).** Mode 2 may run over the network. A route in the server accepts a signed recovery request. The route makes a temporary administrator. This amends ADR 245 decision 12 for this route only. The test of that decision names the route as the one other caller. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	2. **The risk statement (owner).** The documentation states the risk in these words. "A theft of the private key alone gives a bounded remote temporary administrator." The bound is the expiry. The limits of a temporary administrator in ADR 245 also apply. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	3. **The nonce is a unique-key insert.** The server inserts the nonce of a request with a unique key. The insert is in the same transaction that makes the temporary administrator. A second request with the same nonce fails the insert. The transaction fails with it. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	4. **A shared rate limit.** The database holds the counter of the rate limit, so all instances share it. An IPv6 address counts as its /64 group. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	5. **One answer for every refusal.** Every refusal gives the same answer: the same status, the same body and the same shape. A caller cannot tell the reason of a refusal. A bad signature, an old time, a used nonce, a rate limit and a wrong mode look the same. The audit entry holds the real reason. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	6. **The answer token is encrypted.** The server encrypts the token in the answer. It uses an ephemeral public key that the CLI sends in the request. Only the CLI that made the request can read the token. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	7. **Nonce retention.** The server keeps a nonce until the not-after time of its request plus 1 hour. (owner)
docs/adr/0295-break-glass-recovery-mode-2-over-the-network.md	8. **A condition on shipping.** The route ships only after the store keeps the first-boot recovery mode and the key. This is the item "A stored recovery mode" in ADR 245. Until then the route does not exist. (owner)
