docs/adr/0327-the-job-proxy.md	| Owner decision | Decision of this ADR |
docs/adr/0328-the-review-modes-by-milestone.md	1. **Modes 2 and 4 in milestone 5 (owner).** The exit test of milestone 5
docs/adr/0328-the-review-modes-by-milestone.md	2. **Modes 1 and 3 in milestone 6 (owner).** The exit test of milestone 6
docs/adr/0329-placement-rules-match-opentofu-unit-names.md	- This ADR does not decide the syntax of a rule for an OpenTofu unit name, or how it relates to the `project` of the `cloud` block. These points are not part of the owner decision.
docs/adr/0329-placement-rules-match-opentofu-unit-names.md	1. **Placement rules also match OpenTofu unit names (owner).** Placement rules also match OpenTofu unit names.
docs/adr/0329-placement-rules-match-opentofu-unit-names.md	2. **Fail closed (owner).** The server refuses a unit that no rule matches.
docs/adr/0329-placement-rules-match-opentofu-unit-names.md	3. **The OpenTofu HTTP backend (owner).** This includes the OpenTofu HTTP backend.
docs/adr/0331-environment-promotion-is-in-milestone-6.md	1. **RFC 55 is in milestone 6 (owner).** Environment promotion is in milestone 6, on the rule that no feature gets cut.
docs/adr/0331-environment-promotion-is-in-milestone-6.md	2. **RFC 55 is the research (owner).** [RFC 55](../rfc/0055-environment-promotion.md) is the research for this feature.
docs/adr/0331-environment-promotion-is-in-milestone-6.md	3. **The ordered rollout stays later (owner).** The ordered rollout stays later, as ADR 318 decision 3 says.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	1. **SAML sign-in starts at OpenDustpan (owner, 2026-11-07).** The normal SAML sign-in is SP-started. The server makes the `AuthnRequest` and checks `InResponseTo`.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	2. **A portal tile is a bookmark (owner, 2026-11-07).** A tile in an identity provider portal is a bookmark to the SP-started URL of OpenDustpan. It starts an SP-started sign-in. It sends no unsolicited assertion.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	3. **IdP-started SAML is a setting (owner, 2026-11-07).** True IdP-started SAML accepts an assertion that has no request. It is a setting of each SAML provider. It is off by default.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	4. **IdP-started SAML has four required checks (owner, 2026-11-07).** When the setting is on, the server accepts an unsolicited assertion only with all of these checks:
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	6. **Group mapping is a rule for each provider (owner, 2026-11-07).** Each provider has a rule: a trusted claim pattern. A claim value that matches the pattern is linked to a group. The link names the provider and the claim value. It never names the group by name alone.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	7. **Explicit single mappings handle exceptions (owner, 2026-11-07).** An explicit mapping links one claim value of one provider to one group. Use it for a value that the pattern does not cover.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	8. **The rules are a sensitive setting (owner, 2026-11-07).** A change to a rule or to an explicit mapping is a sensitive setting. It writes an audit entry and needs a fresh authentication (step-up), as ADR 70 decision 22 says for a claim mapping.
docs/adr/0332-saml-starts-at-the-service-provider-and-group-claims-map-by-rule.md	9. **SCIM comes later (owner, 2026-11-07).** This ADR adds no SCIM. When SCIM comes, it uses the same mapping, as ADR 320 decision 13 says.
docs/adr/0333-passkey-challenges-in-a-sealed-cookie-and-name-lock-alerts.md	1. **The challenge is a sealed, short-lived cookie (owner, 2026-11-07).** The challenge of a passkey sign-in lives in a sealed cookie with a short life. The server stores no row at the start of the ceremony.
docs/adr/0333-passkey-challenges-in-a-sealed-cookie-and-name-lock-alerts.md	2. **A replay record follows a successful sign-in (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server writes a replay record only after a successful sign-in. A start and a failed response write no record.
docs/adr/0333-passkey-challenges-in-a-sealed-cookie-and-name-lock-alerts.md	3. **This replaces the per-source caps (owner, 2026-11-07).** The caps on open passkey challenges do not apply any more. They were for each source address, for each site group and for the installation. No row holds an open challenge.
docs/adr/0333-passkey-challenges-in-a-sealed-cookie-and-name-lock-alerts.md	4. **A lock that ends has an audit kind (owner, 2026-11-07).** ADR 285 decision 23 gets the audit kind `sign-in.name-lock-ended`. The server writes it when a lock by name ends.
docs/adr/0333-passkey-challenges-in-a-sealed-cookie-and-name-lock-alerts.md	5. **Lock alerts have a ceiling (owner, 2026-11-07).** The alerts of ADR 285 decision 16 have a ceiling for each installation and hour. After the ceiling, the server writes one summary alert for the rest of the hour. The ceiling is 10 alerts for each installation and hour. The summary alert gives the count of alerts left out and the top sources. The ceiling is a setting. The API shows its effective value.
docs/adr/0334-chat-senders-use-the-strict-host-list-and-sinks-write-non-finite-floats-as-text.md	1. **Delete the legacy chat host path (owner, 2026-11-07).** A chat sink has one host rule. Code does not keep a second, weaker path for a sink with a host that the strict list refuses. This applies to the webhook sink too, because both use the same list.
docs/adr/0334-chat-senders-use-the-strict-host-list-and-sinks-write-non-finite-floats-as-text.md	2. **The strict host list applies twice (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server checks the host against the strict list when a person saves the sink. The sender checks it again at send time. The two checks are separate layers. Neither check relies on the other.
docs/adr/0334-chat-senders-use-the-strict-host-list-and-sinks-write-non-finite-floats-as-text.md	4. **The OTLP sink takes `?ca-file=` (owner, 2026-11-07).** The URL of an OTLP sink can have the query option `ca-file`. Its value is the path of a PEM file. The file holds the certificate authorities that the sink trusts for its endpoint. The option adds to the system roots. It does not turn off the check of the certificate.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	1. **A deny gate on a cancel or a force-unlock stays (owner, 2026-11-07).** A `deny` gate at `state.write` can refuse a Pulumi update cancel or a force-unlock. The gate keeps its effect. No rule passes it. The way out is to edit the gate. The edit needs the actions and the fresh authentication that ADR 224 decision 12 gives.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	2. **A break-glass for a gate comes later (owner, 2026-11-07).** A break-glass for a `deny` gate, with a review after use, is a later feature. Until a later ADR accepts it, the break-glass of ADR 224 decision 13 passes the gates in error only.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	3. **An unlocked state version on the TFE-compatible API (owner, 2026-11-07).** Such a write meets `state.write` before its transaction. Its operation kind is `unlocked-write`. A refusal refuses the write. This is the rule that ADR 224 decision 16 already gives for the HTTP backend. The refusal can leave resources that no stored state records. The record and the error say so, as they do for the HTTP backend.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	4. **A held lock outlives a new gate (owner, 2026-11-07).** A lock that is held when a gate is saved is not changed by that gate. A new gate applies at the next lock.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	5. **A save needs a flag when locks are held (owner, 2026-11-07).** Locks can be held on units in the scope of a gate. Then a save of the gate needs an explicit flag in the request. Without the flag, the save answers 409 and changes nothing. The answer lists the held locks: for each lock, the unit, the holder and the time. With the flag, the save goes on. The audit entry names the held locks.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	6. **Docs and UI make this very obvious (owner, 2026-11-07).** The user documentation for gates has a section on held locks. The console shows the held locks before a person saves a gate. It also shows that the gate does not apply to them.
docs/adr/0335-gate-decisions-deny-gates-held-locks-and-the-engine-port.md	7. **The gate engine port stays pluggable (owner, 2026-11-07).** The port `gate.Engine` of ADR 224 decision 9 stays the one place where an engine plugs in. Cedar is the default engine. OPA, Expr or another engine stays possible as an alternative adapter of the same port. An adapter must run the conformance suite `pkg/gate/gatetest`. A new adapter needs its own ADR, as ADR 224 decision 9 says.
docs/adr/0336-the-github-app-is-the-author-of-a-write-back-commit.md	1. **The App is the author, and the person is a co-author (owner, 2026-11-07).** A write-back commit on GitHub has the GitHub App as its author. The message has a `Co-authored-by` trailer that names the person who asked. The job record and the audit entry stay the record of who asked and why.
docs/adr/0336-the-github-app-is-the-author-of-a-write-back-commit.md	2. **GitHub signs the commit (owner, 2026-11-07).** The adapter makes the commit with `createCommitOnBranch`. GitHub signs it. The server holds no signing key for a write-back commit.
docs/adr/0336-the-github-app-is-the-author-of-a-write-back-commit.md	3. **The write-back follows a moved head at most 3 times (owner, 2026-11-07).** The head of the branch can move after the base. Then the write-back may read the files again at the new head. It may try again. It makes at most 3 retries.
docs/adr/0336-the-github-app-is-the-author-of-a-write-back-commit.md	4. **A retry has two conditions (owner, 2026-11-07).** The write-back retries only when both are true:
docs/adr/0337-the-last-released-lock-is-in-the-workspace-api-and-not-in-the-inventory.md	1. **`lastRelease` moves to the workspace API (owner, 2026-11-07).** The API of the workspace shows the record of the last released lock. The record holds the lock ID, the holder, the releaser, the time and the kind, `normal` or `forced`. The inventory entry of a unit does not have the field.
docs/adr/0337-the-last-released-lock-is-in-the-workspace-api-and-not-in-the-inventory.md	2. **The rule of ADR 298 does not change (owner, 2026-11-07).** The record is still kept on the workspace row. It still matches for 24 hours after the release. A repeated unlock still uses it. Only the API that shows it changes.
docs/adr/0338-the-namespace-service-principal-holds-a-stored-grant.md	1. **The permission is a stored grant (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server makes a stored grant in the same transaction that makes the namespace. The grant is for the namespace service principal. The grant gives the principal the action `unit:run-plan` on its own namespace. The creator of the grant is the creator of the namespace. The grant has the time of the namespace creation.
docs/adr/0338-the-namespace-service-principal-holds-a-stored-grant.md	2. **A person can list and revoke the grant (owner, 2026-11-07).** The grant shows in the list of grants like each other grant. A holder of the right to manage access can revoke it. The decision that a trigger starts a plan then names the grant that allowed it.
docs/adr/0338-the-namespace-service-principal-holds-a-stored-grant.md	3. **The principal still holds nothing else (owner, 2026-11-07).** The grant covers the start of a plan only. The principal still has no token and no session. The rule of ADR 267 decision 14 about the read check of the last saver does not change.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	1. **Option B of RFC 117 is accepted (owner, 2026-11-07).** The bootstrap admin token asks for a one-time code for one principal. The person redeems the code in a console session with a fresh sign-in.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	3. **The redeem (owner, 2026-11-07).** The person sends the code in the body of a `POST` in a console session with a fresh authentication. One transaction makes the `site-administrator` grant on the installation, spends the code and stores the marker. It writes an audit entry. The entry names the bootstrap administrator as the creator, and the person and the sign-in ID as the one who took the role.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	4. **The code (owner, 2026-11-07).** The code is 32 bytes from `crypto/rand`. It goes in a `POST` body only. The server never logs it, stores a hash only and compares in constant time. One code is valid at a time, for 10 minutes, for one use, for one role (`site-administrator`) on the installation only.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	5. **The principal (owner, 2026-11-07).** The route accepts only the ID of a live, enabled person of the installation. It also needs a reason with no control or format characters. It refuses a break-glass person, a temporary administrator, a service and a `dev:` subject.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	6. **Fail closed (owner, 2026-11-07).** The redeem checks three facts in the store transaction. The marker is not set. The code names the principal of the session. The authentication of the session is fresh. An unknown state, a set marker or a failed audit write refuses the request. The refusal writes an audit entry.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	7. **The marker is a stored fact (owner, 2026-11-07).** The marker is an installation fact with a read in the API. A later loss of every administrator goes through recovery (ADR 57, ADR 245), not through this route.
docs/adr/0339-the-first-administrator-gets-a-one-time-code-from-the-bootstrap-token.md	8. **The guides change with the code (owner, 2026-11-07).** Three guides change in the pull request that builds the route. They are console sign-in, local users and workload identity.
docs/adr/0340-measure-background-blob-families-by-total-job-time.md	1. **Background families are measured by total job time (owner, 2026-11-07).** Accept the background cost. A background blob family is measured by the total time of its job. This changes ADR 281 decision 2 for these families.
docs/adr/0340-measure-background-blob-families-by-total-job-time.md	2. **Hot paths keep the per-operation measure (owner, 2026-11-07).** A path where a person or a CLI waits keeps the per-operation measure. ADR 281 decision 2 stays for these paths.
docs/adr/0340-measure-background-blob-families-by-total-job-time.md	3. **The record states the job (owner, 2026-11-07).** For each background family, the benchmark record states these items:
docs/adr/0340-measure-background-blob-families-by-total-job-time.md	4. **A miss goes to the owner (owner, 2026-11-07).** A family that misses its limit goes back to the owner with the numbers.
docs/adr/0341-a-taint-that-reaches-a-secret-source-is-a-routing-change.md	1. **The taint reaches an export key (owner, 2026-11-07).** Any change whose taint reaches a secret source, in any export key, is a routing change. It needs `profile:open` as well as `profile:write`. This holds for a save, a rollback, a tag move and the apply of a draft. It also holds for the referrers of the changed profile, as ADR 202 decision 2 says for blocks.
docs/adr/0341-a-taint-that-reaches-a-secret-source-is-a-routing-change.md	2. **An unknown export key fails closed (owner, 2026-11-07).** An export key can be unknown to the server. Then each change under that key is a routing change. A key becomes known only when an ADR lists it. The code that computes the taint must also handle it.
docs/adr/0342-gates-read-state-facts-for-three-more-writes.md	1. **Three writes fill the state facts (owner, 2026-11-07).** For these writes, the server sets `state.available` to `true` and fills `state.resources` and `state.removed`:
docs/adr/0342-gates-read-state-facts-for-three-more-writes.md	2. **The server reads the body with limits (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server reads the new state from the body before the gate runs. The read has a size limit, a depth limit and a time limit. The limits are settings. The API shows their effective values. The server refuses a request that is over a limit.
docs/adr/0342-gates-read-state-facts-for-three-more-writes.md	3. **The read fails closed (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server may fail to read the body or to count the resources in it. Then it does not set `state.available` to `true`. It refuses the write, and the error names the reason. It never allows the write with empty facts.
docs/adr/0342-gates-read-state-facts-for-three-more-writes.md	4. **Counts follow the earlier rule (owner, 2026-11-07).** `state.resources` counts resource instances, and `state.removed` names instance addresses, as for the restore of a state version.
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	1. **A tool import is a runner job (owner, 2026-11-07).** An import that needs the CLI of a tool runs as a runner job. Examples are `tofu state pull` and `pulumi stack export`. The CLI does not run in the server.
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	2. **A bulk import runs in the server (owner, 2026-11-07).** A bulk import reads another backend directly. It keeps the rule of ADR 241 decision 2: it runs in the server, in durable operations.
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	3. **Sweeps run on a runner (owner, 2026-11-07).** ADR 164 decision 1 stays.
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	4. **A pool may have more than one purpose (owner, 2026-11-07).**
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	5. **Converters (owner, 2026-11-07).** A converter from a cloud inventory service to the sweep report format runs on a runner pool that has the sweep purpose.
docs/adr/0343-runner-work-imports-sweeps-and-the-runner-repository.md	6. **The runner repository is an exception for the runner only (owner, 2026-11-07).** The runner lives in its own repository, as ADR 282 decision 4 says. No other component gets a repository of its own from this exception.
docs/adr/0344-an-agent-drafts-a-change-order-and-a-person-confirms-it.md	1. **An agent drafts a change order (owner, 2026-11-07).**
docs/adr/0344-an-agent-drafts-a-change-order-and-a-person-confirms-it.md	2. **A person confirms it (owner, 2026-11-07).** The draft is not a change order until a person confirms it.
docs/adr/0344-an-agent-drafts-a-change-order-and-a-person-confirms-it.md	3. **The rest of decision 14 stays (owner, 2026-11-07).** An agent still records no verdict. It does not attach a plan, approve, request changes, reject or close. It does not resolve or reopen a thread. It does not ask for an apply.
docs/adr/0345-an-artifact-version-has-four-states.md	1. **Four states (owner, 2026-11-07).** The state of an artifact version is `published`, `deprecated`, `retracted` or `revoked`.
docs/adr/0346-a-unit-moves-between-collections-never-between-namespaces.md	1. **No one-click move between namespaces (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server has no one-click move of a unit to another namespace. Such a move would break grants. A person exports the unit and imports it into the other namespace, as ADR 47 decision 7 says.
docs/adr/0346-a-unit-moves-between-collections-never-between-namespaces.md	2. **A person can move between collections (owner, 2026-11-07).** A person can move a unit to another collection of the same namespace. The authorization model must allow it (ADR 51 decision 6).
docs/adr/0347-warn-do-not-block-for-choices-that-the-operator-owns.md	1. **Warn, do not block (owner, 2026-11-07).** When the operator of an installation makes a risky choice about something that the operator owns, the server warns. It does not block the choice. The warning names the risk and the setting to change.
docs/adr/0347-warn-do-not-block-for-choices-that-the-operator-owns.md	2. **The personas (owner, 2026-11-07).** The rule in decision 1 is the default for the homelab, startup and platform personas.
docs/adr/0347-warn-do-not-block-for-choices-that-the-operator-owns.md	4. **Security controls keep their own rules (owner, 2026-11-07).** This ADR does not change a security control. Authentication, authorization, secrets and the audit log keep the rules of their own ADRs, in every mode.
docs/adr/0348-the-console-is-responsive-and-has-no-work-only-for-phones.md	1. **Responsive designs (owner, 2026-11-07).** A console design is responsive. One design works on all screen sizes.
docs/adr/0348-the-console-is-responsive-and-has-no-work-only-for-phones.md	2. **No phone-only work (owner, 2026-11-07).** The project does not do design or build work that serves only phones.
docs/adr/0349-the-openpgp-library-is-also-allowed-in-the-server-registry.md	1. **The server registry may use the OpenPGP library (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server registry may use the OpenPGP library of ADR 316 decision 2. It uses the library to check signatures of private providers and modules.
docs/adr/0349-the-openpgp-library-is-also-allowed-in-the-server-registry.md	2. **The same versions (owner, 2026-11-07).** The versions of ADR 316 decision 2 apply. A new version or a new library needs a new approval.
docs/adr/0350-opendustpan-runners-replace-hcp-terraform-agents.md	2. **A TFE agent token is refused (owner, 2026-11-07).** A TFE agent token is long-lived and has many uses. A runner join token has one use and a short life (ADR 133 decision 2). When the server serves the routes of the TFE agent tokens, a request to create a token fails. The answer is a JSON:API error with a clear message. The message names the route that creates a join token: `POST /pools/{pool}/join-tokens` of the runner API. The documentation says why the token is refused.
docs/adr/0350-opendustpan-runners-replace-hcp-terraform-agents.md	4. **The agent pool routes are not decided (owner, 2026-11-07).** The pool management routes (level 1a) stay undecided. A later RFC covers them. Until the RFC is accepted, ADR 254 decision 4 holds: the server does not serve the TFE agent pool API.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	- **Ownership is a fact (owner, 2026-11-07).** Every package has an `ownership` fact with one of two values. `first-party` means that the organization built and published the package in its own private registry or namespace. `third-party` means public code or other outside code. In the regulated sense, third-party code is SOUP.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	- **The administrator may apply strict rules to first-party packages (owner, 2026-11-07).** This is a choice, as in decision 2. The administrator writes a gate that reads `ownership`.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	- **The defaults differ (owner, 2026-11-07).** A first-party package goes through the review flow that the team already has. That flow is the review request and the gates of the team. A first-party package has no provenance policy by default. A third-party package goes through the provenance policy of the administrator. The example policies follow this split.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	2. **The administrator writes the policy (owner, 2026-11-07).** The policy is a gate over those facts, with the gate model of ADR 222 to ADR 230. An administrator puts it at the installation, the namespace or the unit. The administrator decides which vendors, organizations, registries and versions to trust. The administrator decides what each signal means. The administrator decides which external checks a package needs.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	3. **Guidelines and example policies (owner, 2026-11-07).** OpenDustpan ships guidelines and example policies for each kind of organization. They are data that an administrator copies and edits. They are not defaults. The kinds are a startup, an enterprise with an allowlist, and a regulated organization. The regulated organization has a SOUP process and a record of each approved item.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	4. **No magic (owner, 2026-11-07).** Each allow and each deny names the policy and the facts that it read. The API shows the effective policy. A signal from outside the installation is an unverified fact. The documentation says which signals an attacker can fake, for example stars. A fact shows its origin, the host that gave it and the time of its read.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	5. **Integrity is code (owner, 2026-11-07).** The server serves only the bytes that a person admitted. It checks each hash, each size limit and each signed URL. This is not a view on provenance. An administrator cannot turn it off.
docs/adr/0351-opendustpan-computes-provenance-signals-and-administrators-write-the-policy.md	6. **One mechanism, two kinds of package (owner, 2026-11-07).** The same facts and the same gates serve every package. This covers the public packages of RFC 93, the private packages of ADR 269 and RFC 101, and the sweep plugins of RFC 123. The distinction between public and private packages is a fact and a default. It is not a second mechanism. The owner's words:
docs/adr/0353-gates-at-every-step-of-an-untrusted-plan-as-opt-in-settings.md	4. **Accepted questions (owner, 2026-11-07).** The owner accepted the recommendation of each of the 12 questions of the RFC, as the settings of decision 1:
docs/adr/0353-gates-at-every-step-of-an-untrusted-plan-as-opt-in-settings.md	5. **The rest of the proposal (owner, 2026-11-07).** The rest of the proposal of the RFC applies as settings of decision 1. It has these items:
docs/adr/0353-gates-at-every-step-of-an-untrusted-plan-as-opt-in-settings.md	6. **Security rules stay (owner, 2026-11-07).** A control that a team turns on is never weaker than the rules of ADR 250 in `CLAUDE.md`. A setting can turn a control off only by an explicit stored value that has a record of who set it and when.
docs/adr/0354-agent-principals-delegation-and-the-kind-agent.md	1. **The person who delegated may confirm (owner, 2026-11-07).** Question 1. The person who delegated (Alice) may confirm a change that her own agent proposed, by default. This is the confirmation step of ADR 125 and ADR 128. Four-eyes comes from an approval rule that counts Alice as the author: with `authorMayApprove: false` on the namespace, a second person must approve. A one-person installation keeps working.
docs/adr/0354-agent-principals-delegation-and-the-kind-agent.md	2. **Revocation during an apply (owner, 2026-11-07).** Question 2. A phase that the person confirmed finishes, because the person saw and allowed that exact plan. No new phase starts. Every phase that was not confirmed stops at its next call. This is configurable: an installation setting holds the behavior, with the recommended value as the default. The API shows the effective value.
docs/adr/0354-agent-principals-delegation-and-the-kind-agent.md	3. **The lifetimes of a delegation (owner, 2026-11-07).** Question 3. The default lifetime is 7 days, and 30 days at most, for a public client. It is 30 days and 90 days for Dusty and for confidential clients. This is configurable: an installation setting holds the lifetimes, with these values as the default. The API shows the effective values.
docs/adr/0358-automatic-drift-remediation-is-opt-in-and-a-namespace-can-opt-out.md	1. **Auto-dedrift is available and opt-in (owner, 2026-10-21).** The server
docs/adr/0358-automatic-drift-remediation-is-opt-in-and-a-namespace-can-opt-out.md	Status: accepted · 2026-11-07. Records the owner decision of 21 October 2026 on automatic drift remediation
